  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "4pRmukn799eHY2TtPHAJ2eeuJwk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:35:28.795Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "A drive is a document, and its model type `powerhouse/document-drive` defines a set of *structural* actions: `ADD_FILE`, `ADD_FOLDER`, `MOVE_NODE`, `DELETE_NODE`. Every one of them is an operation appended to **the drive's** operation log \u2014 never to the log of the file being added, moved, or removed.\n\nThis is the part that trips people up. When you create a document, you dispatch `ADD_FILE` against the drive:\n\n```ts\n// This action is dispatched on the drive, not on the new document\naddActions({\n  documentId: \"<drive-id>\",\n  actions: [{\n    type: \"ADD_FILE\",\n    input: {\n      documentType: \"my-org/todo-list\",\n      name: \"Shopping list\",\n      parentFolder: null,\n    },\n  }],\n});\n```\n\nThe new document exists because the *drive's* reducer processed that action. The document's own log starts empty.\n\nThe symmetry holds on the way out. Removing a file appends `DELETE_NODE` to the drive; the removed document's log ends wherever it ended before removal. It has no way to record its own departure, and there is no separate audit document \u2014 the drive's log *is* the record of every structural change. Reasoning about \"which log records this?\" has one answer: membership and layout live on the drive, content lives on the file.\n\nOne precision worth keeping: `DELETE_NODE` removes drive *membership*. Deleting the underlying document is a distinct base action, `DELETE_DOCUMENT` (`packages/shared/document-model/types.ts`), which targets the document itself. Dropping a node from a drive is not the same operation as destroying the document.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/03-document-models/01-documents-and-drives.mdx, confirmed against packages/shared/document-model/types.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:35:27.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:35:28.795Z"
    }
  },
